with almost 500 sessions at next 2018  it's understandable that you can get to  them all so if you didn't see hybrid and  open services with GCP envoy and sto  then stay tuned because here's the  rewind one of the challenges with making  modern cloud native applications is the  amount of strain that I can put on your  DevOps teams this is why we need strong  tools to do monitoring discovery fault  tolerance and observability for micro  service deployments no matter where they  run in this session we brought together  folks from Google cloud and lyft to  share what we've done together in this  space first let's talk about sto an open  source service mesh that makes it easy  to create a network of deployed services  with load balancing service to service  authentication monitoring and more  without any changes in service code this  is especially useful when transitioning  from monolithic applications to a  distributed micro service architecture a  few miles down the road from Google  ride-sharing company lyft was facing  similar challenges with their move to  cloud native as they built micro  services in multiple languages it became  much harder to create a common proxy to  support each language so they developed  envoy an open source distributed proxy  for large service mesh architectures  giving devops eventually consistent  service discovery and observability even  for micro services written in different  languages envoy used very similar api's  to internal Google api's so it became a  natural fit to build it into sto SDO is  comprised of a data plane and a control  plane envoy provides the data plane with  a set of intelligent proxies to control  all network communication between micro  services while the control plane manages  the proxies to route traffic and enforce  policies this now makes it possible to  make a service available to a single  Google kubernetes cluster and across  multiple clusters so with SEO &amp; envoy we  get load balancing across micro services  in the same region but we still need the  ability to balance between regions as  well thankfully traffic director is here  to help  it's a GCP managed service that uses  open api to integrate with sto and  envoys service proxy  to give you global load-balancing  four-year back ends this is really  awesome because you can now expand  services and spill over across regions  it gives you centralized health checking  meaning each proxy doesn't need to check  one another lastly it enables traffic  driven auto scaling for your  containerized backends traffic moved  from one micro service in one region to  the next service in a different region  acting like a global load balancer but  for your own internal services now your  developers can continue to focus on  business logic without a PhD in  provisioning curious to hear more of the  discussion between lift and Google cloud  check out the full talk and hear more  from our panel with the link in the  description below and don't forget to  subscribe to the cloud YouTube channel  for more great next rewind content  thanks for watching  you  [Music]
 

